# rfitops.cf -- t_mds.c coverage: rfit/rfit1 ("Fit of binary
# relation(s)", undocumented in tda.hlp, found via source header
# comments -- both share the literal same syntax box text, another
# instance of the project's recurring stale-doc pattern, this time
# a full duplicate rather than a wrong keyword). Both fit the
# "closest" relation (via linear programming) satisfying requested
# structural properties (rel=: 1 reflexive, 2 symmetric, 3
# antisymmetric, 4 transitive, 5 complete) to a directed, valued
# graph. Requires a VALUED graph specifically ("Error: graph must be
# valued" on an unvalued attempt, confirmed directly) -- gdd(gt=4).
# Graph: edges 1->2, 2->3, 1->3 (a simple asymmetric relation, no
# loops). Verified structurally: the symmetric fit for rel=2
# correctly returns a genuinely symmetric matrix (the diagonal/
# identity solution here, which trivially satisfies the symmetry
# constraint) -- not independently re-derived as the LP's true
# optimum, given the algorithm's own objective isn't documented
# anywhere either.

silent = -1;

nvar(dfile=reledges.dat, I=c1, J=c2, V=c3);
gdd(gt=4) = I,J,V;
rfit(rel=2) = rfitout.txt;
rfit(rel=1,4) = rfitout2.txt;
rfit1(rel=2) = rfit1out.txt;
